WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.560
Tyler: Welcome everyone.

2
00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.000
Tyler: I'm Tyler Yahn.

3
00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:04.822
Nikola: And I'm Nikola Grcevski.

4
00:00:04.822 --> 00:00:10.080
Nikola: Today we're following a slow customer checkout, from the first symptom to a fix.

5
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:14.156
Tyler: The application completes the request, but the customer is waiting.

6
00:00:14.156 --> 00:00:19.789
Tyler: We'll use OBI to find where that time goes, add language detail, and change the

7
00:00:19.789 --> 00:00:20.540
Tyler: code responsible.

8
00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:24.671
Tyler: Then we'll make that visibility available across the application with the

9
00:00:24.671 --> 00:00:27.300
Tyler: OpenTelemetry Injector and RIO, its namespace-driven operator.

10
00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:31.933
Nikola: One customer problem, then a way for the other teams to investigate theirs.

11
00:00:31.933 --> 00:00:33.260
Nikola: Let's open the system.

12
00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:38.043
Tyler: Here is our checkout application: eighteen services, with PostgreSQL and Redis.

13
00:00:38.043 --> 00:00:41.831
Tyler: The Gateway hands the request to Checkout, which calls Inventory and four

14
00:00:41.831 --> 00:00:42.463
Tyler: commerce branches.

15
00:00:42.463 --> 00:00:47.514
Tyler: The map shows what can talk to what; the duration tells us the whole request is

16
00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:47.830
Tyler: slow.

17
00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:51.330
Nikola: Every team can have a different definition of healthy.

18
00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:54.510
Nikola: Following the request gives us one shared starting point.

19
00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:58.195
Tyler: First, OpenTelemetry eBPF Instrumentation, or OBI.

20
00:00:58.195 --> 00:01:02.170
Tyler: We deploy it and let it discover the running applications.

21
00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:07.175
Nikola: That gives us service and dependency observations without setting up a language

22
00:01:07.175 --> 00:01:08.736
Nikola: SDK in each application.

23
00:01:08.736 --> 00:01:12.250
Nikola: We can start investigating while the applications keep running.

24
00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:13.590
Tyler: OBI is ready.

25
00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.290
Tyler: Let's open a checkout request.

26
00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:21.876
Nikola: The useful result is a connected trace.

27
00:01:21.876 --> 00:01:24.770
Nikola: This is the request behind the duration we started with.

28
00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:32.692
Tyler: Now we can see service calls, database work, and how their timings fit together.

29
00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:35.008
Tyler: But a connected trace is not a faster checkout.

30
00:01:35.008 --> 00:01:36.294
Tyler: The customer is still waiting.

31
00:01:36.294 --> 00:01:38.610
Tyler: We need to find the part we can change.

32
00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:42.884
Tyler: Watch the plain topology turn into an observed request path.

33
00:01:42.884 --> 00:01:47.019
Tyler: We can now ask which calls happened, which services handled them, and where the

34
00:01:47.019 --> 00:01:47.610
Tyler: time went.

35
00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:51.617
Nikola: Here we're following the Cart branch through Catalog, Recommendations, and

36
00:01:51.617 --> 00:01:51.986
Nikola: Promotions.

37
00:01:51.986 --> 00:01:56.410
Nikola: This is one branch of the same request moving through the system.

38
00:01:57.730 --> 00:01:59.341
Tyler: Start with the waterfall.

39
00:01:59.341 --> 00:02:04.176
Tyler: Gateway's long bar includes its wait for Checkout, and Checkout includes its

40
00:02:04.176 --> 00:02:04.981
Tyler: downstream work.

41
00:02:04.981 --> 00:02:09.010
Tyler: The longest parent bar doesn't tell us what to fix.

42
00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.259
Nikola: Look at this failed connection from Checkout instead.

43
00:02:12.259 --> 00:02:16.653
Nikola: It consumes about a hundred and fifty milliseconds before the successful product

44
00:02:16.653 --> 00:02:18.850
Nikola: lookup and the other service calls.

45
00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:24.111
Tyler: Now we have somewhere specific to investigate: why is Checkout spending time on

46
00:02:24.111 --> 00:02:25.262
Tyler: a connection that fails?

47
00:02:25.262 --> 00:02:29.291
Tyler: OBI has exposed the failed attempt; language detail can connect it to the method

48
00:02:29.291 --> 00:02:30.730
Tyler: and fallback in our code.

49
00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.330
Nikola: Let's open Checkout and follow that connection.

50
00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:37.210
Tyler: We have source access here.

51
00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:38.458
Tyler: Pricing runs before Inventory.

52
00:02:38.458 --> 00:02:43.138
Tyler: We'll use the Java agent to time that method and expose the library calls inside

53
00:02:43.138 --> 00:02:43.450
Tyler: it.

54
00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:45.822
Nikola: This is OpenTelemetry's zero-code instrumentation.

55
00:02:45.822 --> 00:02:51.157
Nikola: The agent supplies the instrumentation; we still need to make it available, add

56
00:02:51.157 --> 00:02:54.850
Nikola: the startup setting, and configure where its data goes.

57
00:02:58.170 --> 00:02:59.566
Tyler: Here's the deployment change.

58
00:02:59.566 --> 00:03:01.660
Tyler: The application image stays the same.

59
00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:06.196
Tyler: We provide the agent, set the service identity and exporter, and enable the

60
00:03:06.196 --> 00:03:06.894
Tyler: pricing-method span.

61
00:03:06.894 --> 00:03:08.290
Tyler: Then we roll Checkout.

62
00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:09.988
Nikola: Zero code is useful.

63
00:03:09.988 --> 00:03:12.401
Nikola: It hasn't made the release calendar disappear.

64
00:03:12.401 --> 00:03:17.225
Nikola: Someone still needs the runtime knowledge, the settings, and a way to deploy the

65
00:03:17.225 --> 00:03:17.570
Nikola: change.

66
00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:21.923
Tyler: Here's a fresh request with the Java agent.

67
00:03:21.923 --> 00:03:22.681
Tyler: Open PricingEngine.calculate.

68
00:03:22.681 --> 00:03:26.852
Tyler: The long interval contains a failed HTTP connection, then a successful

69
00:03:26.852 --> 00:03:27.610
Tyler: product-price lookup.

70
00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:30.932
Nikola: Open the failed HTTP span.

71
00:03:30.932 --> 00:03:33.335
Nikola: Its error type is Java's SocketTimeoutException.

72
00:03:33.335 --> 00:03:36.139
Nikola: The exception event says, Connect timed out.

73
00:03:36.139 --> 00:03:38.141
Nikola: That is a connection timeout.

74
00:03:38.141 --> 00:03:42.547
Nikola: The product-price lookup after it succeeds, so Checkout completes, but the

75
00:03:42.547 --> 00:03:45.350
Nikola: customer pays for the failed attempt first.

76
00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:47.087
Tyler: OBI got us to Checkout.

77
00:03:47.087 --> 00:03:50.203
Tyler: The language agent exposed the failed attempt and the working fallback.

78
00:03:50.203 --> 00:03:52.470
Tyler: Now we have a code change to investigate.

79
00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:54.674
Tyler: There it is.

80
00:03:54.674 --> 00:03:57.689
Tyler: Pricing still calls a legacy backend that no longer exists.

81
00:03:57.689 --> 00:04:01.608
Tyler: The connection times out after a hundred and fifty milliseconds, then we read

82
00:04:01.608 --> 00:04:03.718
Tyler: the price from the current product store.

83
00:04:03.718 --> 00:04:06.130
Tyler: The fix is to use that store directly.

84
00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:07.916
Nikola: Instrumentation didn't fix the code.

85
00:04:07.916 --> 00:04:09.382
Nikola: It enabled us to act.

86
00:04:09.382 --> 00:04:13.781
Nikola: If we can collaborate with the owning team, we can bring a pull request instead

87
00:04:13.781 --> 00:04:16.419
Nikola: of a vague report that their service is slow.

88
00:04:16.419 --> 00:04:20.818
Nikola: Here is the customer impact, here is the trace showing the timeout, and here is

89
00:04:20.818 --> 00:04:23.750
Nikola: a change that removes it while keeping the price lookup.

90
00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:26.102
Tyler: We're deploying that application change now.

91
00:04:26.102 --> 00:04:29.150
Tyler: The Java agent and exporter settings stay the same.

92
00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.173
Nikola: The rollout is ready.

93
00:04:45.173 --> 00:04:48.230
Nikola: Let's send another customer request and look at its trace.

94
00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.396
Tyler: Here is the new request's waterfall.

95
00:04:51.396 --> 00:04:52.740
Tyler: Expand Checkout and PricingEngine.calculate.

96
00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.756
Tyler: The product-price query is still here.

97
00:04:54.756 --> 00:04:59.460
Tyler: The failed legacy call is gone, and the rest of the request starts sooner.

98
00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:03.900
Nikola: Follow it through Inventory, the commerce branches, and the order write.

99
00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.773
Nikola: We kept the useful work.

100
00:05:05.773 --> 00:05:10.642
Nikola: The customer gets a faster checkout because we're no longer waiting for a

101
00:05:10.642 --> 00:05:12.140
Nikola: backend that isn't there.

102
00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:16.990
Tyler: This is the payoff: a customer symptom, an observed cause, a code change, and a

103
00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:18.500
Tyler: fresh trace showing what changed.

104
00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:24.062
Tyler: In a matched run, the median customer request fell from about three hundred and

105
00:05:24.062 --> 00:05:26.188
Tyler: sixty-two milliseconds to two hundred and fourteen.

106
00:05:26.188 --> 00:05:29.833
Tyler: Pricing fell from about a hundred and fifty-seven milliseconds to about four.

107
00:05:29.833 --> 00:05:32.870
Tyler: The repeated-request result supports what we saw in the waterfall.

108
00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:35.840
Nikola: That summary supports the trace we just saw.

109
00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.153
Nikola: It is a local demo comparison, not a production benchmark.

110
00:05:39.153 --> 00:05:43.790
Nikola: Next, how do we make the same diagnostic detail available to the other teams?

111
00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:46.487
Nikola: We've fixed this checkout.

112
00:05:46.487 --> 00:05:50.330
Nikola: But getting to that answer needed runtime knowledge, configuration, access, and

113
00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:51.029
Nikola: a deployment.

114
00:05:51.029 --> 00:05:55.570
Nikola: How do the other teams get this detail without repeating all that setup?

115
00:05:55.890 --> 00:05:59.698
Tyler: In this demo, thirteen more services use runtimes in the Injector payload.

116
00:05:59.698 --> 00:06:01.919
Tyler: The Go services keep their OBI coverage.

117
00:06:01.919 --> 00:06:05.410
Tyler: Our next step is to share the setup across those thirteen.

118
00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:08.090
Nikola: And share the routine work around it.

119
00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:12.810
Nikola: Not every team has our access, our runtime knowledge, or an empty release queue.

120
00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:17.724
Tyler: To see what we're centralizing, here's the upstream Operator's opt-in

121
00:06:17.724 --> 00:06:18.084
Tyler: configuration.

122
00:06:18.084 --> 00:06:21.678
Tyler: Java and Python have different injection annotations, with an Instrumentation

123
00:06:21.678 --> 00:06:23.116
Tyler: resource supplying their settings.

124
00:06:23.116 --> 00:06:26.710
Tyler: You can put those annotations on workloads or a namespace.

125
00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:28.734
Nikola: That already saves work.

126
00:06:28.734 --> 00:06:33.419
Nikola: RIO, the Runtime Instrumentation Operator from OTelX, takes a different route:

127
00:06:33.419 --> 00:06:38.105
Nikola: select the namespace centrally, let the Injector detect supported runtimes, and

128
00:06:38.105 --> 00:06:40.661
Nikola: let the controller manage existing workloads.

129
00:06:40.661 --> 00:06:46.624
Nikola: This demo uses the bundled prototype that led to RIO, not an upstream Operator

130
00:06:46.624 --> 00:06:47.050
Nikola: installation.

131
00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:51.059
Tyler: Here we install the namespace-driven controller once.

132
00:06:51.059 --> 00:06:55.284
Tyler: This is the demo's RIO precursor, working with the OpenTelemetry Injector.

133
00:06:55.284 --> 00:06:59.125
Tyler: The controller selects workloads and manages rollout; the Injector detects

134
00:06:59.125 --> 00:07:01.430
Tyler: supported runtimes and loads their agents.

135
00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:05.206
Nikola: The payload, exporter, and exclusions are shared settings.

136
00:07:05.206 --> 00:07:09.958
Nikola: Application owners don't each choose a language annotation and arrange a

137
00:07:09.958 --> 00:07:11.254
Nikola: separate instrumentation rollout.

138
00:07:11.254 --> 00:07:14.710
Nikola: Next comes the application choice: instrument this namespace.

139
00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:18.660
Nikola: Here is the selection rule: instrument demo-apps.

140
00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:20.931
Nikola: We select the application boundary once.

141
00:07:20.931 --> 00:07:25.853
Nikola: Unlike the language opt-in we just showed, this rule doesn't list languages or

142
00:07:25.853 --> 00:07:26.610
Nikola: individual services.

143
00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:34.103
Tyler: Apply that rule, and the controller rolls the selected workloads.

144
00:07:34.103 --> 00:07:37.859
Tyler: The Injector detects their runtimes and supplies the agents.

145
00:07:37.859 --> 00:07:42.450
Tyler: Compatible services deployed into the namespace later inherit the same policy.

146
00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:58.764
Nikola: Now all the supported services are lit up.

147
00:07:58.764 --> 00:08:02.950
Nikola: Checkout keeps its direct agent, thirteen more services gain language detail,

148
00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:05.613
Nikola: and the four Go services keep OBI.

149
00:08:05.613 --> 00:08:09.418
Nikola: The ongoing responsibility lives with the platform, instead of thirteen

150
00:08:09.418 --> 00:08:10.940
Nikola: application teams repeating it.

151
00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:16.645
Tyler: Back in the application view, we're looking at a new request through the

152
00:08:16.645 --> 00:08:17.321
Tyler: instrumented fleet.

153
00:08:17.321 --> 00:08:21.716
Tyler: We still have the system view, and now we can open language-level observations

154
00:08:21.716 --> 00:08:22.730
Tyler: in more services.

155
00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:24.196
Nikola: Let's follow Catalog.

156
00:08:24.196 --> 00:08:28.017
Nikola: These are the Java agent's database spans from that service.

157
00:08:28.017 --> 00:08:33.366
Nikola: The same enrollment also covers the Node, Python, Ruby, and dot net services in

158
00:08:33.366 --> 00:08:34.130
Nikola: this application.

159
00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:38.537
Tyler: The namespace rule got us here without a separate instrumentation change from

160
00:08:38.537 --> 00:08:39.559
Tyler: each application team.

161
00:08:39.559 --> 00:08:40.922
Tyler: Checkout keeps its fix.

162
00:08:40.922 --> 00:08:45.349
Tyler: Now the other teams have the language detail ready for their next investigation

163
00:08:45.349 --> 00:08:45.690
Tyler: too.

164
00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:49.144
Tyler: Think about the direct setup we watched.

165
00:08:49.144 --> 00:08:52.192
Tyler: We knew the runtime, had access, and controlled the release.

166
00:08:52.192 --> 00:08:55.850
Tyler: How much happens before someone in a real organization can even begin?

167
00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:57.410
Nikola: Find an owner.

168
00:08:57.410 --> 00:08:58.650
Nikola: Explain the request.

169
00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:02.783
Nikola: A ticket, a meeting, access, review, and a release slot.

170
00:09:02.783 --> 00:09:06.090
Nikola: Then somebody switches away from their feature work.

171
00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:10.202
Tyler: One namespace choice can remove repeated enrollment changes and keep shared

172
00:09:10.202 --> 00:09:10.892
Tyler: settings consistent.

173
00:09:10.892 --> 00:09:13.650
Tyler: The saving is more than typing fewer commands.

174
00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:16.459
Nikola: It is fewer people arranging the same work.

175
00:09:16.459 --> 00:09:19.570
Nikola: Count their active effort separately from the time spent waiting.

176
00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:22.862
Tyler: And setup is only the first bill.

177
00:09:22.862 --> 00:09:23.989
Tyler: A new service arrives.

178
00:09:23.989 --> 00:09:25.398
Tyler: An agent needs an update.

179
00:09:25.398 --> 00:09:27.370
Tyler: A team needs to turn instrumentation off.

180
00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:30.953
Nikola: Compatible new services inherit the namespace choice.

181
00:09:30.953 --> 00:09:33.749
Nikola: Updates reuse the shared rollout path.

182
00:09:33.749 --> 00:09:37.944
Nikola: Configuration, documentation, and recovery instructions have one place to

183
00:09:37.944 --> 00:09:38.410
Nikola: improve.

184
00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:41.190
Tyler: That can reduce drift and repeated troubleshooting.

185
00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:43.650
Tyler: But the platform does not maintain itself.

186
00:09:43.970 --> 00:09:44.442
Nikola: Exactly.

187
00:09:44.442 --> 00:09:47.746
Nikola: Compatibility, rollout, and recovery still need people.

188
00:09:47.746 --> 00:09:51.050
Nikola: Their time goes into the calculation too.

189
00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:54.463
Tyler: Let's price that difference in ownership.

190
00:09:54.463 --> 00:09:58.300
Tyler: This is a hypothetical year, at a hundred dollars per person-hour.

191
00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:01.090
Tyler: Thirteen compatible services remain after our common pilot.

192
00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:06.661
Nikola: With distributed ownership, allow two and a half hours per team to arrange and

193
00:10:06.661 --> 00:10:07.786
Nikola: deploy its instrumentation.

194
00:10:07.786 --> 00:10:10.787
Nikola: Thirteen teams means thirty-two and a half hours.

195
00:10:10.787 --> 00:10:12.288
Nikola: RIO centralizes the setup.

196
00:10:12.288 --> 00:10:17.164
Nikola: Allow one hour for the controller and namespace policy, plus thirty minutes to

197
00:10:17.164 --> 00:10:17.914
Nikola: deploy OBI.

198
00:10:17.914 --> 00:10:19.414
Nikola: Once for the namespace.

199
00:10:19.414 --> 00:10:22.790
Nikola: There is no additional setup allowance for each service.

200
00:10:23.110 --> 00:10:25.436
Tyler: Now assume an agent release every month.

201
00:10:25.436 --> 00:10:29.092
Tyler: Thirteen separately owned updates versus one shared update: a hundred and

202
00:10:29.092 --> 00:10:31.419
Tyler: fifty-six operations versus twelve in a year.

203
00:10:31.419 --> 00:10:35.407
Tyler: At an assumed forty-five minutes each, that's a hundred and seventeen hours

204
00:10:35.407 --> 00:10:36.071
Tyler: versus nine.

205
00:10:36.071 --> 00:10:38.730
Tyler: A hundred and eight hours saved every year.

206
00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:43.564
Nikola: We give neither side a faster-investigation credit once both have equivalent

207
00:10:43.564 --> 00:10:43.974
Nikola: telemetry.

208
00:10:43.974 --> 00:10:49.309
Nikola: OBI gives us broad initial visibility; we count its deployment once, not once

209
00:10:49.309 --> 00:10:50.130
Nikola: per incident.

210
00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:54.253
Tyler: Setup plus monthly upkeep saves a hundred and thirty-nine person-hours in this

211
00:10:54.253 --> 00:10:57.738
Tyler: scenario: thirteen thousand nine hundred dollars of engineering capacity in year

212
00:10:57.738 --> 00:10:58.055
Tyler: one.

213
00:10:58.055 --> 00:11:00.274
Tyler: Ten thousand eight hundred in following years.

214
00:11:00.274 --> 00:11:04.710
Tyler: Replace the assumed hours with your own; this is not a measured customer saving.

215
00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:09.511
Tyler: The same assumptions give about eighty-five thousand dollars of capacity for a

216
00:11:09.511 --> 00:11:13.304
Tyler: hundred services in year one, four hundred and forty thousand for five hundred,

217
00:11:13.304 --> 00:11:15.930
Tyler: and eight hundred and eighty-two thousand for a thousand.

218
00:11:16.250 --> 00:11:18.935
Nikola: These are scenarios, not a scaling law.

219
00:11:18.935 --> 00:11:23.154
Nikola: We retain the demo's supported-runtime mix and allow more independent rollout

220
00:11:23.154 --> 00:11:25.071
Nikola: domains as the fleet grows.

221
00:11:25.071 --> 00:11:29.290
Nikola: Each domain pays for its shared setup and monthly updates once.

222
00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:30.416
Tyler: Already centralized?

223
00:11:30.416 --> 00:11:32.027
Tyler: Compare against that workflow.

224
00:11:32.027 --> 00:11:34.444
Tyler: Equivalent work should give zero saving.

225
00:11:34.444 --> 00:11:38.473
Tyler: Extra compatibility testing, exceptions, hardening, and support need their own

226
00:11:38.473 --> 00:11:38.876
Tyler: estimates.

227
00:11:38.876 --> 00:11:43.710
Tyler: We haven't priced incident response, waiting time, or a smaller telemetry bill.

228
00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:47.738
Tyler: We started with a customer waiting for checkout.

229
00:11:47.738 --> 00:11:49.443
Tyler: OBI showed the failed connection.

230
00:11:49.443 --> 00:11:52.853
Tyler: The Java agent connected it to pricing and its fallback.

231
00:11:52.853 --> 00:11:57.286
Tyler: We removed the stale backend attempt, then returned to the actual request trace

232
00:11:57.286 --> 00:11:58.650
Tyler: to see the change.

233
00:11:58.970 --> 00:12:02.874
